WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.370
We've mentioned a little bit about relevancy already.

2
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.669
I think you've seen some cursor terms coming up a couple of times.

3
00:00:08.669 --> 00:00:14.169
Again, this might be a term or something like this term might exist in other engines,

4
00:00:14.169 --> 00:00:20.170
but I believe we're getting more into the domain now of terminology that might be unique to Unreal.

5
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.170
So you might not be familiar with this exact term, but you probably tackled something like it.

6
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:31.030
Relevancy is essentially the umbrella term used to describe how

7
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.030
and when data is sent to clients.

8
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:40.030
There are essentially rules that we can define whether or not certain data even should make its way to a particular client.

9
00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:46.030
For example, if we're in a game like Player Unknown Battlegrounds or any of these kind of large,

10
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.030
I suppose to be kind of more on brand, I should be saying Fortnite.

11
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:59.030
If we're in a huge world and one client fires a bullet on my 5k by 5k island,

12
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:08.030
if they're on two opposite sides of the island, does client A need to know about client B's bullet being fired?

13
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:14.030
If they're too far away for the sound ever to make their way to that client,

14
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.030
if they're too far away for that bullet ever to possibly hit them,

15
00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:21.030
then it's a fair assumption that it would be irrelevant.

16
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:26.060
And therefore, that's where we can take relevancy into account.

17
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:33.060
And by doing that, by not having to ever replicate any information about that bullet from client B to client A,

18
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.060
then we can obviously reduce our kind of bandwidth consumption.

19
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.159
That's a big win.

20
00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:43.159
And also, not just about bandwidth, but it's also about our CPU, right?

21
00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:47.159
Our server then doesn't have to perform certain calculations.

22
00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:52.120
It's not having to maintain that object on the other client.

23
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:58.120
If an object is relevant, but then spends a long time out of relevancy, it will be destroyed.

24
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.120
And that's following the same destruction logic that we saw in our second session

25
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.120
when we talked about actors getting destroyed.

26
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:12.120
So that means that when it enters relevancy or re-enters relevancy, it will have its begin play called.

27
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.120
And when it leaves relevancy, it will have its destroy called.

28
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.120
And therefore, in the context of a multiplayer game,

29
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:27.120
actors can have their technically the same conceptual actor could have its begin play and destroy

30
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.120
called multiple times due to entering and leaving relevancy.

31
00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:37.819
As I said kind of before, a lot of these concepts are stitched in the actor.

32
00:02:37.819 --> 00:02:43.819
Actor is our kind of primary in point for a lot of the way that we replicate information over the server.

33
00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:46.819
So relevancy is primarily an actor level thing.

34
00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:53.819
If we have components on an actor, they don't really have a concept of relevancy.

35
00:02:53.819 --> 00:02:57.819
Those components are automatically relevant if the actor that they are attached to is relevant.

36
00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:00.819
And the same thing is going to be true for a lot of this functionality.

37
00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:06.590
So when we talk about managing relevancy and how we actually do that,

38
00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:13.590
the default implementation, so the primary way that Unreal assumes you would do relevancy is going to be distance.

39
00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.590
Simply put, if something is too far away, do we care about it?

40
00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.590
So with the example we gave as a bullet being fired, right, that's a kind of easy example.

41
00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:26.590
If you're too far away to ever hear this, see it or get hit by it, then we can assume it's too far.

42
00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:33.590
So by default, Unreal would consider one and a half kilometers to be too far away to be relevant.

43
00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:41.590
But that is configurable on a, that particular distance functionality is configurable by a value.

44
00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:44.590
It's called net cull distance squared.

45
00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.590
So we can actually just quickly go and see that.

46
00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.590
As we said, most of this stuff is done on actor.

47
00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.590
If I go to net cull, you can see this is the value.

48
00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.590
This is a squared distance, obviously for mathematical optimization sake.

49
00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:04.590
So obviously this doesn't look anything like 1.5K, but if you take 1,500 centimeters and times it by 1,500 centimeters,

50
00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.590
you would arrive at this value.

51
00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:13.590
So just be aware that this is a squared value and therefore square root your value to get in centimeters,

52
00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:19.589
how many, or unreal units, which essentially match the map to centimeters, how many units this is going to be.

53
00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:30.230
And yeah, there's a couple of instances where we would actually want to entirely forgo this check.

54
00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:35.230
And that's going to be very global actors that might be kind of game systems.

55
00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:41.230
There's certain actors that would typically want to do this on things like the game state would be obvious examples where it's like,

56
00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:44.230
at what point would the game state not always be relevant to you?

57
00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:48.230
Like everyone should need to know the kind of global information about the game, right?

58
00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:52.230
So the game state is going to be a typical example of something that is going to be always relevant.

59
00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:58.230
And that means that we're not going to do any of these distance based relevancy checks or any kind of overrides that we can do.

60
00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:01.230
It's just we're just going to say, oh, rather than doing relevancy checks,

61
00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:06.000
this thing is always relevant and therefore not even going to check that.

62
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Similarly, we can also, there's kind of additional filtering we can do.

63
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.000
We can say that this thing is always relevant to the person who owns it.

64
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.000
So that may, you know, it's regardless of how far away from it, it's a little bit like always relevant,

65
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.000
but it's only relevant to a particular client connection.

66
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.000
So this is only going to be relevant to a particular player controller or whatever.

67
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.000
And then in some circumstances, you know, maybe distance isn't what we need.

68
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.000
Maybe we've broken our game up into some kind of like partitioning logic.

69
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Maybe we have, based off some kind of clever game logic,

70
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.000
we know that this thing is only going to be relevant when we're looking at it.

71
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.000
So, you know, distance is not going to be the metric that we want to use.

72
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:54.000
If we want to utilize that, we can actually override this distance based functionality with a function called isNetRelevantFor.

73
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.000
And what that essentially says is we kind of establish a relationship between actor A and actor B.

74
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.000
We say, is this actor A relevant for actor B?

75
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.000
And then we can do whatever functionality we kind of need to do there.

76
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:16.220
Yeah, for example, maybe an actor is only relevant to actors of the same team.

77
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:23.220
If we're on a red versus blue and we've got an actor that is, I don't know, our teams,

78
00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:28.220
something that manages UI for notifying our teammates when our flag has been captured

79
00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:31.220
or a flag has been picked up by an enemy team member.

80
00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.220
Obviously, the enemy team doesn't actually need to know about that actor.

81
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.220
That might be a case that you want to override isNetRelevantFor,

82
00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:42.220
and therefore it's not owned by a single one of those connections.

83
00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:46.220
So we can't rely on only relevant to owner, but we can say,

84
00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:53.220
oh, this team manager actor is always relevant for anyone who's on the same team.

85
00:06:55.949 --> 00:07:03.949
And also whilst that's the primary example of when we would be relevant or irrelevant is based off distance,

86
00:07:03.949 --> 00:07:07.949
there is also a kind of shortcut that Unreal would make that it's worth bearing in mind

87
00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:13.949
that if an actor is hidden, which is an Unreal concept of being hidden,

88
00:07:13.949 --> 00:07:16.949
and if an actor is hidden and has its collision turned off,

89
00:07:16.949 --> 00:07:20.949
then it will also be kind of perceived as standard as irrelevant.

90
00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:24.949
So just worth bearing that in mind if you're hiding your actors and turning their collision off

91
00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:28.949
that your network will by default perceive that as irrelevant.
